AB  - In Digital Image Steganography, Pixel-Value Differencing (PVD) methods use the difference between neighboring pixel values to determine the amount of data bits to be inserted. The main advantage of these methods is the size of input data that an image can hold. However, the fall-off boundary problem and the fall in error problem are persistent in many PVD steganographic methods. This results in an incorrect output image. To fix these issues, usually the pixel values are either somehow adjusted or simply not considered to carry part of the input data. In this paper, we enhance the Tri-way Pixel-Value Differencing method by finding an optimal pixel value for each pixel pair such that it carries the maximum input data possible without ignoring any pair and without yielding incorrect pixel values. © 2018 World Scientific Publishing Company.
